      "costUsd": 0.008896,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended with this question. A town of twenty thousand people grows by five per cent each year. How many people after six years? A common answer uses the sixth term, twenty thousand times one point zero five to the five. But count the growth steps. After one year, there's been one. After six years, there have been six. So it's twenty thousand times one point zero five to the six, which is about twenty-six thousand, eight hundred. The start, at time zero, is the first term, so six years later is the seventh term. How do we set up models like this without getting the count wrong?"
    },
